death they say is the only certainty in  life but how and when to die these are  questions that modern medicine gives us  more control over than ever before  as a renowned neurosurgeon henry marsh  dealt more in life and death than most  now as a patient he knows all too well  the fate that may await him i am as you  said an informed consumer i've got  advanced prostate cancer it's in  remission at the moment but ironically  enough i must have operated upon  hundreds of men with advanced prostate  cancer which had spread to the spine and  this causes progressive paralysis so a  large number of men with prostate cancer  die  paralyzed doubly incontinent bedridden  i want some choice in the matter  assisted dying is currently illegal in  the uk the last attempt to change that  via a bill in the house of lords failed  in april shortly afterwards a petition  to legalize it reached a hundred  thousand signatures it will be debated  in the house of commons later today  and there's evidence of a striking  mismatch between public opinion and that  of mps a yougov poll last year found  that whilst 73 percent of people thought  assisted dying should be legalized for  the terminally ill just 35 of mps agreed  others argue that the answer lies not in  changing the law but in improving  palliative care the health service can  and estimates are that 320 people a day  don't have the right end-of-life care  and that's really shocking many of the  heartbreaking stories that we hear  driving the assisted suicide debate are  of people who sadly did not get the  right palliative care and did not have  access to the best support at the time  of their dying or their loved ones dying  either way if the law is to change it  will require more than petitions it will  require government will something that  until now has been absent for good or  for ill matthew thompson sky news
 

